### Why is a single-deployment migration dangerous?

The central mistake is treating a migration as one deployment: change the schema, flip the code, run a big backfill. At hundreds of millions of rows, that either requires downtime or breaks in production.

```java
package com.bank.migration;

public class SingleDeployDanger {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("WRONG: one big deploy");
        System.out.println("  1. ALTER TABLE (locks table for hours)");
        System.out.println("  2. Deploy new code (old code incompatible)");
        System.out.println("  3. Backfill 500M rows in one UPDATE (blows transaction log)");
        System.out.println("  Result: downtime or corruption");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("CORRECT: four separable phases");
        System.out.println("  Each phase leaves a fully working system");
        System.out.println("  Each phase is independently reversible");
    }
}
```

```text
WRONG: one big deploy
  1. ALTER TABLE (locks table for hours)
  2. Deploy new code (old code incompatible)
  3. Backfill 500M rows in one UPDATE (blows transaction log)
  Result: downtime or corruption

CORRECT: four separable phases
  Each phase leaves a fully working system
  Each phase is independently reversible
```

### Phase 1: additive schema change

Introduce the new representation without removing anything the current application depends on. Use online, non-blocking DDL.

```sql
-- Phase 1: additive only. Old app never references balance_minor.
ALTER TABLE accounts ADD COLUMN balance_minor BIGINT NULL;
CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_accounts_balance_minor ON accounts(balance_minor);
-- Old application continues working, completely unaware of the new column.
```

```java
package com.bank.migration;

public class Phase1Schema {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Added balance_minor column (nullable, no default)");
        System.out.println("Created index CONCURRENTLY (no table lock)");
        System.out.println("Old application: completely unaffected");
    }
}
```

```text
Added balance_minor column (nullable, no default)
Created index CONCURRENTLY (no table lock)
Old application: completely unaffected
```

### Phase 2: dual-write code

Deploy code that writes to both representations on every change. Reads still come from the old representation. This step is reversible.

```java
package com.bank.migration;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class Phase2DualWrite {

    static void updateBalance(String accountId, BigDecimal newBalance) {
        // Old representation (decimal)
        System.out.println("Old: UPDATE accounts SET balance = " + newBalance
                + " WHERE id = '" + accountId + "'");

        // New representation (integer paise)
        long paise = newBalance.movePointRight(2).longValueExact();
        System.out.println("New: UPDATE accounts SET balance_minor = " + paise
                + " WHERE id = '" + accountId + "'");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        updateBalance("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("75000.50"));
    }
}
```

```text
Old: UPDATE accounts SET balance = 75000.50 WHERE id = 'SAV2001'
New: UPDATE accounts SET balance_minor = 7500050 WHERE id = 'SAV2001'
```

### Phase 3: throttled, restartable backfill

Backfill historical data in bounded batches with checkpointing, adaptive throttling, and observability.

```java
package com.bank.migration;

public class Phase3Backfill {

    static long checkpoint = 0;

    static void backfill(long totalRows, int batchSize) {
        long processed = 0;
        for (long id = checkpoint; id < totalRows; id += batchSize) {
            long end = Math.min(id + batchSize, totalRows);
            System.out.println("Batch: rows " + id + " to " + end);
            processed += (end - id);
            checkpoint = end;

            // Adaptive throttle: check production latency
            boolean highLatency = (processed % 50_000 == 0); // simulated
            if (highLatency) {
                System.out.println("  Production latency high, pausing 5s...");
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Backfill complete. Checkpoint: " + checkpoint);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        backfill(100_000, 10_000);
    }
}
```

```text
Batch: rows 0 to 10000
Batch: rows 10000 to 20000
Batch: rows 20000 to 30000
Batch: rows 30000 to 40000
Batch: rows 40000 to 50000
  Production latency high, pausing 5s...
Batch: rows 50000 to 60000
Batch: rows 60000 to 70000
Batch: rows 70000 to 80000
Batch: rows 80000 to 90000
Batch: rows 90000 to 100000
  Production latency high, pausing 5s...
Backfill complete. Checkpoint: 100000
```

### Phase 4: verify and staged cutover

Continuously verify old and new representations agree. Only when verification is clean, move reads behind a feature flag, then writes, then drop the old column much later.

```java
package com.bank.migration;

public class Phase4Cutover {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int sampleSize = 10000;
        int mismatches = 0;

        System.out.println("Verification: sampled " + sampleSize + " rows");
        System.out.println("Mismatches: " + mismatches);
        System.out.println();

        if (mismatches == 0) {
            System.out.println("Step 1: Move READS to new representation (feature flag)");
            System.out.println("Step 2: Observe for 1 week");
            System.out.println("Step 3: Move WRITES exclusively to new representation");
            System.out.println("Step 4: Drop old column in separate deployment (weeks later)");
        } else {
            System.out.println("STOP: investigate mismatches before cutover");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Verification: sampled 10000 rows
Mismatches: 0

Step 1: Move READS to new representation (feature flag)
Step 2: Observe for 1 week
Step 3: Move WRITES exclusively to new representation
Step 4: Drop old column in separate deployment (weeks later)
```

### Why does staging lie about migration performance?

In staging, the backfill has the database to itself. In production, it competes for CPU, disk I/O, buffer pool, row locks, and replication bandwidth.

```java
package com.bank.migration;

public class StagingVsProduction {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("=== Staging ===");
        System.out.println("Buffer cache: empty, all for backfill");
        System.out.println("Lock contention: none (no live traffic)");
        System.out.println("Replication lag: zero");
        System.out.println("Backfill time: 1 hour");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("=== Production ===");
        System.out.println("Buffer cache: shared with live queries");
        System.out.println("Lock contention: live transfers compete for row locks");
        System.out.println("Replication lag: backfill generates write load");
        System.out.println("Backfill time: 12+ hours (must throttle for live traffic)");
    }
}
```

```text
=== Staging ===
Buffer cache: empty, all for backfill
Lock contention: none (no live traffic)
Replication lag: zero
Backfill time: 1 hour

=== Production ===
Buffer cache: shared with live queries
Lock contention: live transfers compete for row locks
Replication lag: backfill generates write load
Backfill time: 12+ hours (must throttle for live traffic)
```

### What are common mistakes?

Running one giant UPDATE over hundreds of millions of rows, which holds locks, bloats the transaction log, and starves live queries.

```sql
-- WRONG: one giant update
UPDATE accounts SET balance_minor = balance * 100;
-- Holds locks on 500M rows, transaction log bloats, live queries time out

-- CORRECT: bounded batches with checkpoint
-- UPDATE accounts SET balance_minor = balance * 100
--   WHERE id BETWEEN ? AND ? AND balance_minor IS NULL;
-- Repeat in batches of 5000, checkpoint after each
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How do you handle the case where the dual-write fails on one representation but succeeds on the other?
- How do you make the cutover instantly reversible?
- What happens if a new bug is found after cutting over reads but before cutting over writes?
- How do you estimate the backfill duration in production?
